00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
ottawa has been outsourcing its visa  application process for more than a  decade from a few centers in india in  2007 to today with more than 160  processing centers in more than 100  countries around the world  they are all run by a for-profit company  called vfs global it is headquartered in  dubai but it's owned by a swedish  investment firm its current contract  with canada is worth more than 300  million dollars and it has similar visa  processing contracts with dozens of  other countries so when you actually  look at the impact on the system it  allows us to  potentially welcome thousands and  thousands of more people to canada than  otherwise would be the case but the  ukraine crisis has opened up some cracks  in the outsourcing system canada relies  on with complaints about service levels  long lineups and delays conservative mp  jazran hallan toured a vfs global center  during a recent trip to europe i saw the  system there myself i was a little bit  underwhelmed at how much better canada  can be doing today but immigration  minister sean fraser says the  humanitarian crisis like the one in  ukraine is proving the worth of canada's  decision to outsource last month vfs  global opened up what it called pop-up  visa processing centers on ukraine's  border to deal with the surge of  applicants on behalf of its clients  including canada and when you have the  opportunity to use either pop-up or  existing visa application centers in  addition to permanent locations uh for  uh for local missions uh you really can  respond to humanitarian crises more  effectively ndp immigration critic jenny  kwan has long worried about the privacy  implications for those who used  outsourced visa application centers well  to be very honest with you i don't like  the idea at all  with visa application centers there's a  lot of sensitive information that goes  out our main concern is that we should  be bringing things back to canada then  bringing back more to home and have more  control over of course given the rise of  cyber security but it looks like ottawa  is going in the opposite direction the  government is looking at plans to boost  its reliance on these outsourced visa  application centers and increase the  services they provide  david aiken global news ottawa
 

